nodecode-loci is a rewrite of AskLoci's AskLoci/loci-plugins:

  https://github.com/AskLoci/loci-plugins
  commit 51dca9ab524b7a7953292d2d70ace87bf97ed5ec

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Cozu LLC (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the whole plugin. The original is a Hermes Agent plugin
made of no code at all: a manifest pointing at the Loci app's own
streamable-HTTP MCP server on loopback (with the app's discovery file
answering the URL and the token), and two skills of prose guidance for the
tools that server advertises. This cell is Common Lisp: it reads the same
section-shaped facts (endpoint, token, a token file), discovers the token in
the same discovery file, speaks the same JSON-RPC over the image's own HTTP
call, and reaches the same tools with the same arguments -- loci_status,
loci_generate, loci_map, loci_files, loci_extract, loci_search, loci_index,
loci_transcribe -- through three model tools (loci_status, loci_call,
loci_generate), a /loci command and the (help :loci) topic. The two skills'
guidance lives in that one primer and in the tool descriptions. Nothing is
fetched at run time and there is no Python.

What was left out: the MAC-only installer walk-through (the pinned disk image,
its SHA-256 check and the /reload-mcp step), because a cell is installed by
the organism and re-read by (restart-cells); the harness's own approval
prompt for write-capable calls, which a cell's tools do not carry; and the
Hermes-only manifest fields (app-bundle presence, plist version, liveness
polling), of which the cell keeps the one that matters: the discovery file
the app writes. A tool the app does not advertise is not invented here: the
cell lists what the running app admits to and proxies to that.
